  "final_answer_preview": "After an accident, your rights under the Motor Vehicles Act include the protection from liability if the act was done without criminal intent and with proper care. Specifically, Section 18 states that nothing is an offence if it is done by accident or misfortune without criminal intention while performing a lawful act. This means that if you were involved in an accident but acted with caution and care, you may not be held liable for the incident. Additionally, Section 30 provides that actions taken in good faith for the benefit of another person, even without their consent, are not considered an offence, provided they do not intentionally cause death or serious harm. This can apply in emergency situations where immediate action is necessary. Furthermore, Section 39 clarifies that the right to defend oneself does not extend to causing death but does allow for causing harm to an assailant under certain conditions.",
